<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizhny Novgorod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1221">1221</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Gorky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nizhny Novgorod is a city at the confluence of the Oka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, founded in 1221 by Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II of Vladimir</a>. In the 14th century it was the capital of the Grand Principality of Nizhny Novgorod-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> before passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1392. It later became a great trading centre and was called Gorky from 1932 to 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod.jpg" alt="Nizhny Novgorod" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexTref871 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nizhny_Novgorod_2025-04-29_Minin_and_Pozharsky_square_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nizhny Novgorod stands at the confluence of the Oka and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, 420 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. The Oka divides it into two parts: the Upper City on the hilly right bank, which includes the Kremlin, the historical and administrative centre, and the Lower City on the low western side, built up around the Gorky Automobile Plant. Originally the name was just Novgorod, meaning Newtown. To tell it from the older Novgorod to the west, it was called Novgorod of the Lower Lands, lower because it lay downstream of cities such as Moscow, Vladimir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city began as a small wooden hillfort founded by Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II of Vladimir</a> in 1221 at the confluence of two of the most important rivers of his principality. It was the easternmost Russian settlement until 1372. After its incorporation into the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1392, the local princes took the name Shuysky and settled in Moscow. The Crimean Tatar chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> burned the city in 1408; it was restored and regarded by the Muscovites as a great stronghold against the Tatars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>.</p><p>The red-brick Kremlin, built in 1508-1511 under Pietro Francesco, withstood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> sieges in 1520 and 1536. In 1612 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuzma-minin">Kuzma Minin</a> and Prince Dmitry Pozharsky organised an army in the city for the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>.</p><p>In 1817 the Makaryev Fair was transferred to Nizhny Novgorod and began to draw millions of visitors a year, so that by the middle of the 19th century the city was the trade capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. At the All-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> exhibition of 1896, Alexander Popov's radio receiver and Vladimir Shukhov's hyperboloid tower were demonstrated. In the Soviet period the city became an important industrial centre, known as Gorky and nicknamed the Russian Detroit after the Gorky Automobile Plant. Shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> it took the name Nizhny Novgorod again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nizhny Novgorod is the administrative centre and largest city of Nizhny Novgorod Oblast and of the Volga Federal District, with about 1.2 million residents at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its metro opened in 1985, and the city marked its 800th anniversary on 21 August 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nizhny Novgorod” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its princes 'gathered the Russian lands' by purchase, inheritance, marriage, diplomacy and war, absorbing <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> in 1392, Yaroslavl in 1463, Rostov in 1474, the Novgorod Republic in 1478, Tver in 1485, Vyatka in 1489, Pskov in 1510 and Ryazan around 1521.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate's trade made Kazan's fair the main fair of the Volga region until, in 1525, Safa Giray agreed at Russian insistence to move it to <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Grand Duchy of Moscow, Tver, <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> and other principalities of north-eastern Russia were all formed from its appanages, and the Moscow princes counted their descent from Vsevolod the Big Nest through Alexander Nevsky.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1221 Grand Prince Yuri II founded <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> as a forward base against them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In New York, artists such as Arshile <b>Gorky</b>, Jackson Pollock and Robert Motherwell converged closely with the exiled Surrealists, albeit with some suspicion and reservations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/surrealism">Surrealism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The composer Mily Balakirev wrote the song down from Nikolay Aleynikov in <b>Nizhny Novgorod</b> in 1860 or 1861, and published it with his own arrangement in his collection of Russian folk songs in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li></ul></section>
